{"title":"The Constitution of Kenya, 2010","promulgationDate":"2010-08-27","totalChapters":18,"totalArticles":264,"articles":[{"articleNumber":1,"chapterNumber":1,"chapterTitle":"SOVEREIGNTY OF THE PEOPLE AND SUPREMACY OF THIS CONSTITUTION","partTitle":null,"title":"Sovereignty of the people","content":"(1) All sovereign power belongs to the people of Kenya and shall be exercised only in accordance with this Constitution.\n(2) The people may exercise their sovereign power either directly or through their democratically elected representatives.\n(3) Sovereign power under this Constitution is delegated to the following State organs, which shall perform their functions in accordance with this Constitution--\n(a) Parliament and the legislative assemblies in the county governments;\n(b) the national executive and the executive structures in the county governments; and\n(c) the Judiciary and independent tribunals.\n(4) The sovereign power of the people is exercised at--\n(a) the national level; and\n(b) the county level.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"All sovereign power belongs to the people of Kenya and shall be exercised only in accordance with this Constitution."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The people may exercise their sovereign power either directly or through their democratically elected representatives."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"Sovereign power under this Constitution is delegated to the following State organs, which shall perform their functions in accordance with this Constitution-- (a) Parliament and the legislative assemblies in the county governments; (b) the national executive and the executive structures in the county governments; and (c) the Judiciary and independent tribunals."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"The sovereign power of the people is exercised at-- (a) the national level; and (b) the county level."}]},{"articleNumber":2,"chapterNumber":1,"chapterTitle":"SOVEREIGNTY OF THE PEOPLE AND SUPREMACY OF THIS CONSTITUTION","partTitle":null,"title":"Supremacy of this Constitution","content":"(1) This Constitution is the supreme law of the Republic and binds all persons and all State organs at both levels of government.\n(2) No person may claim or exercise State authority except as authorised under this Constitution.\n(3) The validity or legality of this Constitution is not subject to challenge by or before any court or other State organ.\n(4) Any law, including customary law, that is inconsistent with this Constitution is void to the extent of the inconsistency, and any act or omission in contravention of this Constitution is invalid.\n(5) The general rules of international law shall form part of the law of Kenya.\n(6) Any treaty or convention ratified by Kenya shall form part of the law of Kenya under this Constitution.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"This Constitution is the supreme law of the Republic and binds all persons and all State organs at both levels of government."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"No person may claim or exercise State authority except as authorised under this Constitution."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The validity or legality of this Constitution is not subject to challenge by or before any court or other State organ."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"Any law, including customary law, that is inconsistent with this Constitution is void to the extent of the inconsistency, and any act or omission in contravention of this Constitution is invalid."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"The general rules of international law shall form part of the law of Kenya."},{"clauseNumber":"(6)","text":"Any treaty or convention ratified by Kenya shall form part of the law of Kenya under this Constitution."}]},{"articleNumber":3,"chapterNumber":1,"chapterTitle":"SOVEREIGNTY OF THE PEOPLE AND SUPREMACY OF THIS CONSTITUTION","partTitle":null,"title":"Defence of this Constitution","content":"(1) Every person has an obligation to respect, uphold and defend this Constitution.\n(2) Any attempt to establish a government otherwise than in compliance with this Constitution is unlawful.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Every person has an obligation to respect, uphold and defend this Constitution."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"Any attempt to establish a government otherwise than in compliance with this Constitution is unlawful."}]},{"articleNumber":4,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"Declaration of the Republic","content":"(1) Kenya is a sovereign Republic.\n(2) The Republic of Kenya shall be a multi-party democratic State founded on the national values and principles of governance referred to in Article 10.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"Kenya is a sovereign Republic."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The Republic of Kenya shall be a multi-party democratic State founded on the national values and principles of governance referred to in Article 10."}]},{"articleNumber":5,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"Territory of Kenya","content":"Kenya consists of the territory and territorial waters comprising Kenya on the effective date, and any additional territory and territorial waters as defined by an Act of Parliament.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"1","text":"Kenya consists of the territory and territorial waters comprising Kenya on the effective date, and any additional territory and territorial waters as defined by an Act of Parliament."}]},{"articleNumber":6,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"Devolution and access to services","content":"(1) The territory of Kenya is divided into the counties specified in the First Schedule.\n(2) The governments at the national and county levels are distinct and inter-dependent and shall conduct their mutual relations on the basis of consultation and cooperation.\n(3) A State organ shall ensure reasonable access to its services in all parts of the Republic, so far as it is appropriate to do so having regard to the nature of the service.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The territory of Kenya is divided into the counties specified in the First Schedule."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The governments at the national and county levels are distinct and inter-dependent and shall conduct their mutual relations on the basis of consultation and cooperation."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"A State organ shall ensure reasonable access to its services in all parts of the Republic, so far as it is appropriate to do so having regard to the nature of the service."}]},{"articleNumber":7,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"National, official and other languages","content":"(1) The national language of the Republic is Kiswahili.\n(2) The official languages of the Republic are Kiswahili and English.\n(3) The State shall--\n(a) promote and protect the diversity of language of the people of Kenya; and\n(b) promote the development and use of indigenous languages, Kenyan Sign Language, Braille and other communication formats and technologies accessible to persons with disabilities.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The national language of the Republic is Kiswahili."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The official languages of the Republic are Kiswahili and English."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The State shall-- (a) promote and protect the diversity of language of the people of Kenya; and (b) promote the development and use of indigenous languages, Kenyan Sign Language, Braille and other communication formats and technologies accessible to persons with disabilities."}]},{"articleNumber":8,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"State and religion","content":"There shall be no State religion.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"1","text":"There shall be no State religion."}]},{"articleNumber":9,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"National symbols and national days","content":"(1) The national symbols of the Republic are--\n(a) the national flag;\n(b) the national anthem;\n(c) the coat of arms; and\n(d) the public seal.\n(2) The national symbols are as set out in the Second Schedule.\n(3) The national days are--\n(a) Madaraka Day, to be observed on 1st June;\n(b) Mashujaa Day, to be observed on 20th October; and\n(c) Jamhuri Day, to be observed on 12th December.\n(4) A national day shall be a public holiday.\n(5) Parliament may enact legislation prescribing other public holidays or providing for the celebration of any public holiday.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The national symbols of the Republic are-- (a) the national flag; (b) the national anthem; (c) the coat of arms; and (d) the public seal."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The national symbols are as set out in the Second Schedule."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"The national days are-- (a) Madaraka Day, to be observed on 1st June; (b) Mashujaa Day, to be observed on 20th October; and (c) Jamhuri Day, to be observed on 12th December."},{"clauseNumber":"(4)","text":"A national day shall be a public holiday."},{"clauseNumber":"(5)","text":"Parliament may enact legislation prescribing other public holidays or providing for the celebration of any public holiday."}]},{"articleNumber":10,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"National values and principles of governance","content":"(1) The national values and principles of governance in this Article bind all State organs, State officers, public officers and all persons whenever any of them--\n(a) applies or interprets this Constitution;\n(b) enacts, applies or interprets any law; or\n(c) makes or implements public policy decisions.\n(2) The national values and principles of governance include--\n(a) patriotism, national unity, sharing and devolution of power, the rule of law, democracy and participation of the people;\n(b) human dignity, equity, social justice, inclusiveness, equality, human rights, non-discrimination and protection of the marginalised;\n(c) good governance, integrity, transparency and accountability; and\n(d) sustainable development.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"The national values and principles of governance in this Article bind all State organs, State officers, public officers and all persons whenever any of them-- (a) applies or interprets this Constitution; (b) enacts, applies or interprets any law; or (c) makes or implements public policy decisions."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The national values and principles of governance include-- (a) patriotism, national unity, sharing and devolution of power, the rule of law, democracy and participation of the people; (b) human dignity, equity, social justice, inclusiveness, equality, human rights, non-discrimination and protection of the marginalised; (c) good governance, integrity, transparency and accountability; and (d) sustainable development."}]},{"articleNumber":11,"chapterNumber":2,"chapterTitle":"THE REPUBLIC","partTitle":null,"title":"Culture","content":"(1) This Constitution recognises culture as the foundation of the nation and as the cumulative civilisation of the Kenyan people and nation.\n(2) The State shall--\n(a) promote all forms of national and cultural expression through literature, the arts, traditional celebrations, science, communication, information, mass media, publications and libraries;\n(b) recognise the role of science and indigenous technologies in the development of the nation; and\n(c) promote the intellectual property rights of the people of Kenya.\n(3) Parliament shall enact legislation to--\n(a) ensure that communities receive a royalty or a fair share of royalties from the use of their cultures and cultural heritage; and\n(b) recognise and protect the ownership of indigenous seeds and plant varieties, their characteristics and their use by the communities of Kenya.","clauses":[{"clauseNumber":"(1)","text":"This Constitution recognises culture as the foundation of the nation and as the cumulative civilisation of the Kenyan people and nation."},{"clauseNumber":"(2)","text":"The State shall-- (a) promote all forms of national and cultural expression through literature, the arts, traditional celebrations, science, communication, information, mass media, publications and libraries; (b) recognise the role of science and indigenous technologies in the development of the nation; and (c) promote the intellectual property rights of the people of Kenya."},{"clauseNumber":"(3)","text":"Parliament shall enact legislation to-- (a) ensure that communities receive a royalty or a fair share of royalties from the use of their cultures and cultural heritage; and (b) recognise and protect the ownership of indigenous seeds and plant varieties, their characteristics and their use by the communities of Kenya."}]}]}